FADE IN:

# TEASER

EXT. BELMONT CREST — THE RIVER GATE — DAWN

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 6:09 AM EST

Gray light over the Chattahoochee. Mist on the water. The iron lantern over the boathouse door still burns.

Down the bluff path, one by one: Mama Dee in her church coat with a thermos. Tobias with his leather portfolio. Saint, yawning, collar up. Amond.

Keisha comes last, hood up, a manila envelope pressed flat against her chest. She hasn't slept. It shows.

Two knocks each.

^INT. THE RIVER GATE BOATHOUSE — THE TABLE — CONTINUOUS

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 6:12 AM EST

The black-lacquered Table. Dawn through the high stone windows. The river loud under the floor.

Six chairs filled. The Hammer's chair is empty, a cell phone on the table in front of it, speaker on. The Eighth Chair, as always, empty.

Marc at the head, in a black sweater. He looks at Keisha.

MARC
Ghost.

KEISHA
It came in at four this morning.
(beat)
Don't ask me how.

MARC
I won't.

She slides the envelope down the table. Marc opens it and takes out a single photocopied page: an order form on the letterhead of KRUGER PRECISION STONEWORKS — JOHANNESBURG. Order number KPS-2004-1187.

KEISHA
A stone-cutting shop. Monuments, corporate awards, gaming tokens for the casinos at Sun City. Founded 1987.
(beat)
In September, a company called Harbour Light Holdings, out of Cape Town, ordered two thousand black onyx discs. Thirty-eight millimeters. Crown and wave.

Marc reads. His eyes stop on one line.

MARC
(reading aloud, quietly)
"Customer instruction: wave to have six crests. Do not correct to seven."

Silence around the Table.

MAMA DEE
(very still)
Do not correct to seven.

KEISHA
The cutter must have looked at the artwork and thought it was a mistake. Somebody told him it wasn't.

TOBIAS
Somebody who knew exactly what ours look like.

SAINT
Everybody on the Southside's seen our coin, Mama. It's sat on half the counters on Metropolitan Parkway.

MAMA DEE
Seen it. Not counted the waves.
(beat)
You'd have to hold one. Close. In good light.

That lands. Nobody says anything for a moment.

MARC
(still reading)
Shipped October twenty-second. Durban to Savannah.
(beat)
The MV Sovereign Dawn.

AMOND
(beat)
That's one of ours. Donohue Shipping. Your grandfather named it in 1999.

MARC
(evenly)
I know what it's called, Mond.

ROMAN (V.O.)
(through the speakerphone, road noise)
I'm on I-16 past Dublin. I'll be at the Customs House when it opens at eight. If that ship brought in a container for Harbour Point, there's a filing with names on it.

TOBIAS
And my deaconess mailed the Chatham microfiche overnight to a UPS Store on Abercorn Street. Roman can pick it up at nine. We'll see who walked in with that bearer letter.

Marc sets the page down in the center of the Table.

MARC
Two thousand.

KEISHA
Two thousand.

MARC
He's used three.

Nobody at the Table says anything. The river runs under them.

MARC (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Nobody buys two thousand of anything to use three.
(beat)
He's planning a war.

MAMA DEE
(beat)
And Silas?

MARC
You tell Silas he gets a name today. Not a company. A name. I promised Friday.

MAMA DEE
Baby, it's six in the morning, and all we have is a company.

MARC
Roman.

ROMAN (V.O.)
Tonight.

MARC
(to Mama Dee)
Tonight.

Mama Dee looks at him a long time. Then she nods, unscrews her thermos, and pours him the first cup.

MAMA DEE
Drink this. You look like a ghost.

KEISHA
(dry)
That's my job.

A small ripple of tired laughter. Marc takes the coffee. He looks down the length of the Table at the Eighth Chair.

MARC
He doesn't get his war.

SMASH CUT TO:

> MAIN TITLES — "KINGDOM" <

===

# ACT ONE

INT. FBI ATLANTA FIELD OFFICE — CONFERENCE ROOM — DAY

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 9:02 AM EST

A federal conference room on Century Parkway: gray carpet, a long table, a whiteboard that reads DEEP WATER, and a framed photograph of the Attorney General.

Doyle sits at the head of the table with a legal pad. Reyes sits beside her with a laptop.

Hughes sits across from them in full uniform, the FBI LIAISON folder closed in front of him. He was early. His coffee is untouched.

DOYLE
Deputy Chief. Welcome to Deep Water.

HUGHES
Special Agent.

DOYLE
When your Chief called last night and told me who she was sending, I laughed so loud my partner thought somebody got shot.

REYES
It was a very loud laugh.

HUGHES
(evenly)
I imagine it was.

DOYLE
She knows it was you.

HUGHES
(beat)
Yes.

DOYLE
And she sent you anyway. Why?

Hughes looks down at the folder for a long moment.

HUGHES
Because she's smarter than me.
(beat)
And because she wants to find out whether I'm a leak or a cop.

DOYLE
Which are you?

HUGHES
(looking up)
I was a cop for twenty-five years before I was anything else, Special Agent. I'd like to be one again.

Doyle holds his gaze. Then she slides a single sheet of paper across the table.

DOYLE
Then carry this back to your Chief. Word for word.

Hughes picks it up. An FBI Laboratory report.

REYES
Quantico ran the park coin. Onyx is banded, and the banding has a chemical fingerprint depending on where it came out of the ground.

DOYLE
Your fake came out of the Northern Cape. South Africa.

HUGHES
(reading)
South Africa.

REYES
Machine-cut. Commercial grade. The real one, the seven-crest coin from the counter at Big E's, is hand-cut. Different stone. Brazilian. Whoever makes the real ones is old-fashioned and very, very good.

Hughes lowers the page.

HUGHES
(quietly)
So she was right.

DOYLE
She was right before Monday.
(beat)
I'd like to know what else she's right about, Deputy Chief. That's what you're for now.

HUGHES
(beat)
And if something I carry ends up on Channel Eight?

DOYLE
(dry)
Then I'll know exactly where it came from, won't I?

A beat. And then Hughes does something neither of them expects. He smiles. Tired and real.

HUGHES
She said you'd like early.

DOYLE
(almost smiling back)
She was right about that too.

CUT TO:

INT. THE PARK TOWER CONDOMINIUMS — TENTH STREET — LOBBY — DAY

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 9:44 AM EST

A sleek Midtown lobby: marble, a small fountain, a wall of brass mailboxes. Through the glass front doors, across Tenth Street, the bare trees of Piedmont Park.

Behind the concierge desk, a black-and-white security monitor and a VCR. The CONDO CONCIERGE (30s), in a blazer, hovers. Jeremy leans over the monitor in his task-force windbreaker.

Elxa pushes in through the front doors in her uniform coat.

ELXA
Tell me.

JEREMY
Eleven days canvassing every building on Tenth with a lens pointed at the sidewalk. This one's lobby camera catches the street through the glass.
(to the Concierge)
Once more, please.

The Concierge presses PLAY.

On the monitor: the lobby, the glass doors, and beyond them a slice of Tenth Street sidewalk under a streetlight. The timestamp reads 01/03/05 18:48.

A figure crosses the frame from left to right, toward the park. Tall. A dark canvas jacket. A baseball cap pulled low. A gym bag over one shoulder.

Four seconds. Then he's gone.

JEREMY (CONT'D)
Six forty-eight. Monday the third. Lamar Whitlock was walking his usual loop around the lake by seven.

ELXA
Again.

The Concierge rewinds. The figure passes again.

ELXA (CONT'D)
(leaning in)
He never looks at the building.

JEREMY
Never looks at anything. Head straight. Same pace the whole way. Arms quiet.

ELXA
(beat)
He knows where the cameras are.

JEREMY
Or he doesn't care, because he knows the cap is enough.

Elxa straightens. Her cell phone buzzes. She glances at it. HUGHES. A beat of surprise. She answers.

ELXA
(into phone)
Jackson.
(listening)
Go ahead, Warren.
(listening, her face sharpening)
The Northern Cape.
(beat)
Machine-cut.
(beat)
Thank you, Deputy Chief.
(beat, a small pause)
No. Thank you.

She hangs up. Jeremy looks at her.

JEREMY
Hughes?

ELXA
The FBI lab says our fake came out of the ground in South Africa. He called me from the parking lot. Before he even drove back.
(beat)
He carried it straight.

JEREMY
(slowly)
Huh.

ELXA
(looking back at the frozen figure on the monitor)
South Africa. A man who walks like he's on a parade ground. A van driver who "moves like a soldier."
(beat)
Jeremy, how many South Africans in Atlanta walk like that?

JEREMY
That we know of?

ELXA
That we can find.

JEREMY
(to the Concierge)
We're going to need to borrow this tape.

CONDO CONCIERGE
(nervous)
The board's going to want a receipt.

ELXA
(pleasantly)
The board is going to get one.

CUT TO:

INT. DONOHUE TOWER — VICTOR'S OFFICE — 88TH FLOOR — DAY

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 10:22 AM EST

The chapel-sized office. The partners' desk. Across Peachtree, the Smilley crown.

Victor sits behind the desk. On the blotter in front of him: the October courier log, open, and Wendell Suttles's fax from Garden City, turned face down.

A knock. Martin leans in, immaculate in a gray three-piece suit, a leather folio under his arm.

MARTIN
You wanted me, Grandfather?

VICTOR
Sit down, Martin.

Martin sits. He crosses one leg over the other. Perfectly at ease.

VICTOR (CONT'D)
(pleasantly)
Harbour Point Logistics.

A beat. Martin doesn't blink. If anything, he looks faintly pleased, like a student who studied for exactly this question.

MARTIN
The customs broker in Garden City. Donohue Shipping bought it in '98 from Gerald Pettaway.
(beat)
I requested the full file in October. For the Strategic Development review. Every Shipping subsidiary. Twenty-two of them. I've been meaning to bring you the binder.

He opens his leather folio. He takes out a thick bound report and sets it on the desk. The cover reads: DONOHUE SHIPPING — SUBSIDIARY PORTFOLIO REVIEW — Q4 2004 — CONFIDENTIAL — M. OLSON.

Victor doesn't touch it.

VICTOR
The file came up by courier. October fourteenth. Somebody signed for it on this floor. "M."

MARTIN
(easily)
Me. I sign everything "M." Mother says it's lazy.
(beat)
And I'll confess something, since you're asking. I had Mrs. Ames put the request on your letterhead. Shipping's people in Savannah don't answer a vice president's memo for six weeks. They answer the Chairman's in two days.
(beat)
I should have asked you first. I'm sorry, Grandfather.

Victor studies him. Martin holds his grandfather's gaze, open and contrite.

VICTOR
(evenly)
And the letter to Chatham Savings?

MARTIN
(beat, genuinely puzzled)
Chatham?

VICTOR
A bank in Savannah. October fifteenth. On my letterhead. With my stamp.

MARTIN
(slowly)
I don't know anything about a bank, Grandfather. I asked for files. I don't write to banks.
(beat, concerned)
Is something wrong?

Victor looks at him for a very long time. Martin waits, perfectly still, perfectly patient.

VICTOR
(finally)
Leave the binder.

MARTIN
(standing)
Of course.
(at the door, turning)
Grandfather. If somebody used your name on something you didn't sign. I'd like to help you find out who.

VICTOR
(beat)
I'm sure you would.

Martin smiles, a small, warm, grandson's smile. He goes. The door closes.

Victor sits alone with the thick bound binder. He opens it to the table of contents. He runs his finger down to "HARBOUR POINT LOGISTICS, LLC — p. 187."

He turns to page 187. Three pages of tidy analysis. Revenue. Headcount. A recommendation: "RETAIN — LOW STRATEGIC VALUE."

It's excellent work. It's exactly what Martin said it would be.

Victor closes the binder.

VICTOR (CONT'D)
(to himself, very quietly)
He had his answer ready.

He picks up the phone. He dials four digits.

VICTOR (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Mrs. Ames. Did my grandson ask you to put a request on my letterhead in October?
(listening)
You don't remember.
(beat)
No. That's all right. Nobody would.

He hangs up. He turns the Suttles fax face up. On the back, in his own hand: MARC. MARTIN.

He doesn't cross either one out.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT TWO

INT. PETER'S MERCEDES — MOVING — I-85 NORTH — DAY

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 11:08 AM EST

A silver Mercedes sedan on the downtown connector, the skyline rising ahead. Peter drives. Jasmine sits in the passenger seat in a camel coat, her Louis Vuitton carry-on in the back, her BlackBerry in her lap.

JASMINE
You look tired.

PETER
I've been waiting up for our daughter.

JASMINE
(beat)
What does that mean?

PETER
It means she came home at three o'clock Thursday morning. From a nightclub. Grace left without her.

JASMINE
(turning to him)
Peter.

PETER
She's fine. Nothing happened. They talked on a roof.

JASMINE
Who's "they"?

PETER
A DJ. He calls himself Q.

JASMINE
Q what?

PETER
(beat)
That's what I asked.

Jasmine sits back. She looks out at the skyline.

JASMINE
She's grounded.

PETER
She's grounded.

JASMINE
(beat)
Good.
(beat, lower)
Peter. Harbour Point.

PETER
(eyes on the road)
What's Harbour Point?

JASMINE
A Donohue Shipping company in Garden City. It's in an FBI file. Operation Deep Water. Somebody's been moving cash through it.
(beat)
That's why I came home early.

PETER
(beat)
Does your father know?

JASMINE
That's what I'm going to find out. Tonight. At dinner. Martin will be there. Martin knows every company Daddy owns.

His cell phone buzzes in the cup holder between them. Jasmine glances at it.

INSERT — THE DISPLAY: "+52 998 ..."

Peter reaches over and silences it without looking.

JASMINE (CONT'D)
(evenly)
Who's that?

PETER
A patient.

JASMINE
From Mexico.

PETER
People come from all over.

JASMINE
(beat)
That's what you said at Christmas.

He doesn't answer. He changes lanes.

JASMINE (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Twenty-four years, Peter. I know when you're lying. You get very, very polite.

PETER
(beat)
Then I'll be rude. It's a patient. It's nothing. Let it go.

Jasmine looks at her husband's profile. She lets it go. For now. She looks out the window.

The phone buzzes again. Neither of them looks at it.

CUT TO:

INT. PROBATE ATTORNEY'S OFFICE — DOWNTOWN — CONFERENCE ROOM — DAY

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 12:31 PM EST

A small, old-fashioned law office in the Healey Building: wood paneling, law books, a window onto Forsyth Street.

At a scarred oak conference table: the PROBATE ATTORNEY (60s), bow tie. LEROY REESE (70s), a dignified old man in his church suit with a cane and a fedora on the table beside him. And Harmony, in charcoal, a pen in her hand and an option agreement in front of her.

PROBATE ATTORNEY
Mr. Reese is the executor for his late brother's estate and speaks for his nephews in Detroit. The two lots at 441 and 443 Auburn Avenue. Six months' exclusive option. Earnest money wired this morning.

HARMONY
Thank you.

LEROY REESE
(to Harmony)
Young lady. Who's buying my brother's land?

HARMONY
Smilley International, sir.

LEROY REESE
(beat)
Not the Donohues?

HARMONY
No, sir.

LEROY REESE
(nodding slowly)
My daddy shined shoes in the lobby of the Hotel Donohue. 1938 to 1951.
(beat)
Mr. Alexander tipped a dollar. Every time. In 1940 a dollar was a day's pay.

HARMONY
(beat)
That's a good story.

LEROY REESE
It's a true one.
(beat)
I'm not selling to the Smilleys because I don't like the Donohues, young lady. I'm selling because my nephews in Detroit want the money and I'm seventy-six years old and tired of paying taxes on grass.
(beat)
But you tell Miss Smilley something for me.

HARMONY
Yes, sir.

LEROY REESE
You tell her Auburn Avenue remembers who tipped.

Harmony looks at him. She nods.

He signs. His hand is slow and careful.

The attorney slides the agreement to Harmony. She signs under "Buyer's Representative." Harmony Divine. It's the first time her name has been on anything bigger than a condo.

She looks at it a moment.

PROBATE ATTORNEY
Congratulations, Ms. Divine.

HARMONY
(barely)
Thank you.

In the hallway afterward, she leans against the old wall and dials her cell.

HARMONY (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Esther. It's signed. Four forty-one and four forty-three.
(listening, laughing)
Yes. My first one.
(beat)
And Esther. Mr. Reese says to tell you Auburn Avenue remembers who tipped.

She listens. Her face changes a little.

HARMONY (CONT'D)
(quietly)
She says, "So does she."

CUT TO:

INT. THE BAKER HOME — 214 MAGNOLIA TERRACE — KITCHEN — DAY

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 1:06 PM EST

The Baker kitchen. Gloria sits at the farmhouse table with a sheet of cream stationery, a fountain pen, and a wastebasket full of crumpled drafts.

Kiara, home from school with a half day, sits across from her eating cereal.

KIARA
That's draft seven.

GLORIA
I know what draft it is.

KIARA
Can I read it?

GLORIA
No.

KIARA
(reaching)
Mama, let me read it. I got an A on my persuasive essay.

Gloria hesitates. Then she slides the sheet across. Kiara reads.

KIARA (CONT'D)
(reading)
"Dear Ms. Divine. I am writing to express my regret for the circumstances under which the court's order was delivered—"
(beat, looking up)
Mama. You sound like a lawyer.

GLORIA
I'm apologizing about a legal document.

KIARA
You're apologizing to a person.
(beat)
She's twenty-two. She's got a baby. And she slapped Amond, which, respect.
(beat)
Say it like you'd say it to me if you embarrassed me at school.

Gloria looks at her daughter.

GLORIA
(beat)
When did you get smart?

KIARA
Ninth grade. You were busy.

Gloria laughs despite herself. She takes a fresh sheet. She writes. She reads aloud as she writes, slowly.

GLORIA
"Dear Harmony.
(beat)
I'm sorry you were served at your desk. I didn't mean for it to happen that way, and if I'd known, I'd have stopped it.
(beat)
I still want to know my granddaughter. I'm not going to pretend I don't. But I never wanted to embarrass her mother.
(beat)
She has your eyes and my son's laugh. I'd give anything to hear it more than eleven times.
(beat)
Gloria Baker."

She stops. Kiara is quiet.

KIARA
(softly)
That's the one.

Gloria folds it. She puts it in an envelope. She writes the address from memory.

GLORIA
(sealing it)
Don't tell your brother I let you edit me.

KIARA
(eating cereal)
Too late. I'm putting it in my away message.

CUT TO:

EXT. SAVANNAH — FACTORS WALK — ABOVE RIVER STREET — DAY

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 1:34 PM EST

Old cobblestones and iron bridges over the Savannah River. Down on the water, a container ship slides upriver toward the port at Garden City, stacked with steel boxes.

Roman Gaines sits on a stone bench in a canvas jacket, a thermos beside him and two manila envelopes on his knee. One is stamped U.S. CUSTOMS AND BORDER PROTECTION — PUBLIC ENTRY RECORD. The other is a UPS envelope from Abercorn Street.

He dials his cell. It rings once.

MARC (V.O.)
(filtered)
Go.

INTERCUT WITH:

^INT. BSI TOWER — MARC'S OFFICE — 58TH FLOOR — CONTINUOUS

The wall of monitors. Marc stands at the window. Amond and Tobias are on the couch, listening, the phone on speaker on the coffee table.

ROMAN
The MV Sovereign Dawn docked at Garden City on November ninth. One container off it was consigned to Harbour Point Logistics. Twenty-foot box. Declared as "stone gaming tokens and monument samples, Republic of South Africa."
(beat)
It cleared customs November eleventh.

MARC
Who filed it?

ROMAN
(beat)
That's why I called instead of waiting.
(reading)
"Customs attorney of record: Bullock and Associates, LLP. Atlanta, Georgia."

Silence in Marc's office. Amond sits forward. Tobias stops writing.

MARC
(very quietly)
That's my father's firm.

ROMAN
Yes.

MARC
Whose name?

ROMAN
No person. The firm's customs power of attorney is on file with the port. It says "Bullock and Associates, by its Savannah associate." The signature's a squiggle.
(beat)
The clerk says the firm's name is on every Donohue Shipping container that comes through here. Thousands of them. It's like finding the port's own name on a box.

TOBIAS
(to Marc)
So it's routine.

MARC
It's routine that makes it perfect.
(beat)
Anybody who pulls that record and doesn't know better sees a South African crate of fake coins, cleared by Robert Bullock's firm, delivered to a Donohue company that paid a dead man.

AMOND
(quietly)
It looks like your father.

MARC
(beat)
It's supposed to.

Nobody speaks.

MARC (CONT'D)
Roman. The other envelope.

ROMAN
(opening the UPS envelope)
Chatham microfiche. December ninth. The sixty-thousand-dollar cash withdrawal. The teller photocopied the ID of whoever carried the bearer letter.
(beat)
It's blurry. Microfiche always is. But the license is readable.
(beat)
New Zealand. The fern in the corner.

MARC
Name.

ROMAN
(reading slowly)
Kerr. Cameron James Kerr. Date of birth 1968.

Tobias writes it down in block capitals.

MARC
The face?

ROMAN
Gray blur. Short hair. A long jaw. Could be a thousand men.
(beat)
But Silas said the Hertz girl saw a New Zealand license.

AMOND
Same man.

MARC
Same license. Maybe not the same name. Nobody who orders two thousand fake coins through a Cape Town shell walks into a bank with his real name on his ID.

ROMAN
No.
(beat)
But it's the name he's using this week.

Marc turns from the window.

MARC
Tobias. Every Cameron Kerr who's entered the United States in the last twelve months.

TOBIAS
Legally?

MARC
(beat)
Gently.

TOBIAS
(nodding)
Gently.

MARC
Roman. Come home. Stop at the Harbour Point office in Garden City on the way. Don't go in. Just look at it. I want to know who works there.

ROMAN
Tonight.

MARC
And Roman.
(beat)
Thank you.

The line clicks off. In Savannah, Roman sits a moment on the stone bench, watching the container ship. Then he tucks both envelopes inside his jacket and stands.

In Marc's office, Amond looks at Marc.

AMOND
You have to tell your father.

MARC
(beat)
Not yet.

AMOND
Marc. Somebody's building a case against him with a crane.

MARC
I know.
(beat)
And if I tell him, he'll ask me how I know. And then I'll have to decide whether to lie to my father.
(beat)
I'd like one more day before I have to decide that.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT THREE

EXT. THE JACKSON HOME — CASCADE HEIGHTS — THE BACK PORCH — DAY

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 2:41 PM EST

The screened back porch. Wind chimes. The space heater glowing. The five-thousand-piece Atlanta skyline puzzle is a little more finished than it was Tuesday. The wrong piece of sky is still where Harold put it.

Harold sits at the card table in a cardigan. The screen door creaks.

DENISE JACKSON (54), warm, church-hat elegant even at home, leans out of the kitchen door.

DENISE
Harold. You have company. And he brought a pie.

Hughes steps onto the porch in his uniform, cap under one arm, a white bakery box in the other hand. He looks like a man walking into a church he hasn't been to in years.

HAROLD
(not getting up)
Warren.

HUGHES
Chief.

HAROLD
I'm not the Chief anymore.

HUGHES
You're the only one I ever called that.

A beat. Harold nods at the other chair. Denise takes the pie box with a look at her husband and goes back inside.

Hughes sits. He looks at the puzzle.

HUGHES (CONT'D)
There's a piece in the wrong place.

HAROLD
I know.

HUGHES
Why don't you take it out?

HAROLD
(beat)
Because I put it there.

Hughes takes that in. Neither of them speaks for a long moment.

HUGHES
(finally)
She gave me the FBI. As liaison.

HAROLD
I heard.

HUGHES
After I leaked her memo.
(beat)
She knows it was me. She called me into her office, closed the door, and said everybody in the building gets one mistake. She said you taught her that.

HAROLD
(quietly)
I taught it to you first.

HUGHES
(beat)
I know.

His voice catches. He looks away at the backyard, at the bare dogwood.

HUGHES (CONT'D)
This morning a federal agent handed me a lab report that proves the Chief was right about the coin from the start. And I drove it straight to her. Didn't call anybody first. Didn't even think about it.
(beat)
It felt like 1988.

HAROLD
Hollywood Road.

HUGHES
I carried that baby out, Harold. I didn't think about who'd get the credit. I just carried her out.

HAROLD
(beat)
That's the cop I made a sergeant.

Hughes nods. He can't talk for a second.

HUGHES
(finally, low)
I thought I'd hate her forever.

HAROLD
And?

HUGHES
(beat)
And she's good. She's better than me. She's twenty-four, and she's better than me.
(beat)
That's the worst part.

HAROLD
No, son.
(beat)
The worst part would've been if she wasn't, and she got it anyway.

Hughes looks at his old mentor. Then, slowly, he laughs. It's shaky, but it's a laugh.

Harold picks up a puzzle piece and holds it out to him.

HAROLD (CONT'D)
Here. That's the Bank of America building. Top floor. Put it somewhere.

Hughes takes it. He studies the puzzle. He finds the spot. He clicks it in.

HUGHES
(beat)
Harold. The Harlow case. In 2000.

Harold's hand stops over the table.

HUGHES (CONT'D)
Your son asked you who told you to close it. The Chief told me.
(beat)
I was your deputy then. I've always wondered too.

A long silence. The wind chimes ring.

HAROLD
(quietly)
Eat your pie, Warren. Denise made coffee.

Hughes looks at him. He doesn't push. He picks up another piece of sky.

CUT TO:

INT. SAINT'S CAR WASH — WEST END — THE OFFICE — DAY

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 3:16 PM EST

A glass-walled office overlooking the wash bays. Suds, spray arms, Hot 97 playing over the speakers. Framed on the wall: SAINT'S CAR WASH — 14 LOCATIONS — "WE FORGIVE ONCE."

Saint sits behind a cluttered desk eating wings out of a Styrofoam box. The door opens without a knock.

Teddy Merriweather stands there in his camel overcoat. Behind him in the bays, a black Escalade sits dripping, half-washed.

TEDDY
You the Saint?

SAINT
(wiping his fingers)
I'm the man who owns this car wash. Sit down, Teddy.

Teddy hesitates at his own name. He doesn't sit.

TEDDY
You had somebody following me.

SAINT
I had somebody eating at the Waffle House. It's a free country.
(beat)
You had lunch with a man who doesn't eat.

TEDDY
I eat with whoever I want.

SAINT
You do. And I watch whoever I want.
(beat)
Sit down. I'm not going to hurt you. I've got wing sauce all over my hands.

After a moment, Teddy sits on the edge of the chair across the desk.

SAINT (CONT'D)
What'd he tell you?

TEDDY
Who?

SAINT
The tall man. With the cap. Who tipped exact.

TEDDY
(beat)
He said your people killed Lamar and Big E. He said my uncle's too old to see it. He said he could help me see it.

SAINT
Help you how?

TEDDY
(beat)
He didn't say yet.

SAINT
He will.
(leaning forward)
Teddy. That man bought two thousand fake coins with our crown on them. He's used three. You know what he needs the other one thousand nine hundred and ninety-seven for?

Teddy says nothing.

SAINT (CONT'D)
He needs somebody from your side of the boulevard to start something. So it looks like a war. So the police and the FBI and the newspapers all look at us and all look at you, and nobody looks at him.
(beat)
He needs a fool, Teddy. He picked you because you're the only one in your family who's young enough to be one.

TEDDY
(standing, flushing)
Watch your mouth.

SAINT
(calmly, not standing)
I'm watching it. I'm watching yours too.
(beat)
Your uncle came to Mama Dee's on his own two feet last Friday for the first time in thirty years. He stood on the church steps with us Saturday. He sent Mama Dee flowers in a hearse, which, I'm not going to lie, was a lot.
(beat)
Don't make your uncle bury you, Teddy. He's buried enough people.

Teddy stares down at him. His jaw works.

TEDDY
(finally)
He's supposed to call me Monday.

SAINT
(beat)
The tall man?

TEDDY
Monday.

SAINT
Then Monday, you answer. And you tell your uncle what he says.
(beat)
Not me. Your uncle. That's all I'm asking.

Teddy looks at him a long moment. Then he turns and walks out through the suds and the spray to his half-washed Escalade.

Saint watches him go. He picks up his phone.

SAINT (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Mama Dee. Tell Silas his nephew's getting a phone call Monday.

CUT TO:

INT. THE DIVINE HOME — CAMPBELLTON ROAD — THE KITCHEN — DAY

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 4:03 PM EST

A small, spotless brick ranch in Southwest Atlanta. A kitchen with yellow curtains, a Formica table with four chairs, and a church calendar. On the counter: three cakes under glass domes. Lemon. Plain. Sweet potato pie.

In the front window, visible from the kitchen doorway, a single white candle in a brass holder, unlit in the daylight.

On the refrigerator, under magnets: DEE's high-school graduation photo. A FIND DEE flyer. A MARTA Route 71 schedule.

CALVIN DIVINE (51), a huge, gentle man in his MARTA uniform with his cap in his hands, sits at the table. LORETTA DIVINE (49), still in Grady scrubs, pours coffee. Harmony sits beside her father in her charcoal suit.

Carla sits across from them with her legal pad, reading glasses on, and a slice of lemon pound cake she's actually eating.

CARLA
(chewing, sincerely)
Mrs. Divine, this is the best pound cake I've had since my mother passed.

LORETTA
(pleased, trying not to be)
It's butter. You can't rush it.

CARLA
No, ma'am.
(setting down her fork, gentler)
Mr. Divine. Tell me about Dee. Not the night. Just Dee.

Calvin turns his cap in his big hands.

CALVIN
(beat)
He laughed loud. Everywhere. Church, the movies, the grocery store. His mama used to pinch him.

LORETTA
Every Sunday.

CALVIN
He fixed people's cars for free. Anybody on this street had a problem, they came to Dee. He was going to open a shop. He had the name picked out. "Divine Intervention Auto."

HARMONY
(a wet laugh)
It was a terrible name.

CALVIN
(smiling at her)
It was a terrible name.

CARLA
(writing)
It's a wonderful name.
(beat)
Mr. Divine. When Dee went out at night, how did he get home?

CALVIN
He called me.
(beat)
Always. Didn't matter how late. He knew I'd come. I'm up at four-thirty for my route anyway.

CARLA
Where did he call from?

CALVIN
The Texaco. Northside and West Paces. Pay phone by the ice machine.
(beat)
If he was up on Northside, he'd walk down to the Texaco and call me, and I'd come get him in the Buick. Fifteen minutes.

Carla stops writing.

CARLA
(carefully)
He'd walk south on Northside. From the 3100 block. To West Paces.

CALVIN
That's the only way down. There's no other road.

CARLA
(beat)
And that night?

Calvin looks at his cap. Loretta puts her hand on his shoulder.

CALVIN
(quietly)
He never called.
(beat)
I sat up in the living room in my uniform till six. Then I drove my route. I looked at every stop.
(beat)
I've been looking at every stop for three years.

Harmony takes her father's hand. Nobody speaks.

Carla writes one line on her legal pad. She underlines it twice.

INSERT — THE LEGAL PAD: "Route: 3100 blk Northside -> SOUTH -> Texaco @ W. Paces. On foot. ~1:45–2:15 AM. Only road."

BACK TO SCENE

CARLA
(gently)
Mrs. Divine. Is there anything of Dee's I could take with me? Something he carried.

Loretta hesitates. Then she gets up and goes down the hall. She comes back with a small black pager, old, scuffed, with a belt clip.

LORETTA
His pager. He left it on his dresser that night. He always forgot it.
(beat)
We've paid the bill every month for three years. In case he calls it.

Carla takes it in both hands, like something breakable.

CARLA
I'll bring it back.

LORETTA
(beat)
Bring him back.

The kitchen is very quiet. Carla looks at this mother.

CARLA
(quietly)
I'll find out what happened to him, Mrs. Divine. I promise you that.
(beat)
I've never broken that one.

Loretta nods. She sits down. She cuts Carla another slice of cake without asking, and Carla eats it.

CUT TO:

INT. MERRIWEATHER & SONS FUNERAL HOME — THE FAMILY PARLOR — DUSK

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 5:32 PM EST

A hushed parlor with wine-red drapes, velvet chairs, a box of tissues on every table, and an arrangement of white lilies in the corner. The room where families sit before a viewing.

Silas sits in a wingback chair in his black suit. Otis stands by the door.

Mama Dee sits across from Silas in her church coat, her purse on her knees. On the table between them: a single index card, face down.

SILAS
Friday.

MAMA DEE
Friday.

SILAS
Your boy promised me a name.

Mama Dee turns the card over and slides it across the table. In her careful schoolteacher's hand: CAMERON JAMES KERR. NEW ZEALAND. B. 1968.

Silas reads it. He puts on his reading glasses and reads it again.

SILAS (CONT'D)
(quietly)
New Zealand.

MAMA DEE
Same license the Hertz girl saw.

SILAS
(beat)
Otis.

OTIS
Sir.

SILAS
Call your cousin at Hertz. Ask her if the name was Kerr.

Otis steps into the hall with a cell phone. Silas sets the card down.

SILAS (CONT'D)
And this Kerr. He's the one who killed Lamar and Earl?

MAMA DEE
He's the one who paid for the coins. And he's the one who took sixty thousand dollars out of a bank in Savannah with a letter that wasn't his, and put twenty thousand in Lamar's account the day before Lamar died. To make it look like somebody paid Lamar for something.
(beat)
And he's the one your nephew had lunch with.

Silas closes his eyes briefly.

SILAS
Teddy.

MAMA DEE
Saint talked to Teddy this afternoon. Without laying a hand on him. The man's calling Teddy Monday.

SILAS
(opening his eyes)
Monday.

MAMA DEE
Teddy's going to tell you what he says.

SILAS
(beat)
Teddy told your man that?

MAMA DEE
Teddy told my man that.

Silas sits back. For the first time all week, something in his face loosens.

Otis comes back in, closing his phone.

OTIS
Kerr, sir. She remembers now. "Like the Irish name."

Silas nods slowly. He looks at the card.

SILAS
Tell your boy something for me, Dolores.

MAMA DEE
I'm listening.

SILAS
Tell him he kept his word. On a Friday.
(beat)
Tell him the truce holds as long as he wants it to.

MAMA DEE
(beat)
And the man?

SILAS
(folding the index card, putting it in his breast pocket beside his silver fork)
When they find him, we share.
(beat)
That's what we said on the church steps.

Mama Dee stands. She pulls on her gloves.

MAMA DEE
You still an old fool, Silas.

SILAS
(standing too, a small bow)
I'm your old fool, Dolores. Since the Royal Peacock.

She pauses at the door. She doesn't turn around, but she smiles.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT FOUR

INT. MANUEL'S TAVERN — NORTH HIGHLAND AVENUE — A BACK BOOTH — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 6:12 PM EST

The legendary Atlanta political bar: dark wood, framed newspaper front pages, campaign posters from 1960 onward, and cops and reporters drinking side by side at the long bar.

In a back booth under a framed photo of Jimmy Carter, Elxa sits in plain clothes with a Coke. Across from her, Doyle, with a Guinness.

DOYLE
You drink Coke in a bar.

ELXA
I'm on call. I'm always on call.

DOYLE
(sipping)
Your deputy chief delivered the lab report this morning. He called you from our parking lot before he was out of the space.

ELXA
I know. I was on the other end.

DOYLE
(beat)
I've been doing this job twelve years. I've seen a lot of chiefs handle a lot of leaks. I've never seen anybody turn one into a delivery service.

ELXA
He's a good cop. He forgot for a week.

DOYLE
(studying her)
You really believe that.

ELXA
His mentor believes it. I trust his mentor.

Doyle turns her glass on the table.

DOYLE
I didn't come here to talk about Hughes.

ELXA
I didn't think you did.

DOYLE
(beat)
You showed me yours on Monday. The three coins. You gave them up without a fight. Now I'll show you mine.
(lowering her voice)
Deep Water has a name on it that's going to be very hard for you to hear, Chief.

ELXA
(evenly)
Donohue Shipping.

DOYLE
(beat, surprised)
How long have you known?

ELXA
My aunt's a congresswoman with friends at Justice. My grandmother told me over a plate of pound cake.
(beat)
And your lab says the stone came from South Africa. And two weeks ago a van driver who "moves like a soldier" drove a van with stolen church plates to Piedmont Park.
(beat)
This morning I watched a tall man in a cap walk past a condo lobby toward the park at six forty-eight, the night Lamar Whitlock died. He walks like he's on a parade ground.

She slides a VHS tape across the table in a plastic evidence sleeve.

ELXA (CONT'D)
That's a copy. You can keep it.

Doyle looks at the tape. Then at Elxa.

DOYLE
(quietly)
You're giving me evidence. In a bar.

ELXA
I'm giving you a copy of evidence. Over a Coke. Because I'd rather have you in the tent than outside it.
(beat)
And because if this road runs through my family's shipping company, I want a federal agent standing next to me when I walk down it. So nobody can ever say a Donohue granddaughter looked away.

A long beat. Doyle picks up the tape and puts it in her bag.

DOYLE
(finally)
Military.

ELXA
That's what my husband said.

DOYLE
Your husband's right. I was Army before the Bureau. Two years at Fort Bragg. I know that walk.
(beat)
Somebody trained him. And somebody's paying him.

ELXA
Then we find out who's paying.

Doyle raises her Guinness an inch. Elxa raises her Coke.

DOYLE
Truce?

ELXA
(beat)
Truce.

They drink. At the bar, a WBKG cameraman on his night off glances over at the booth, sees who it is, and looks away politely.

DOYLE
(setting down her glass)
For what it's worth, Chief. When I read your file, I thought you were a Donohue princess who got a badge for Christmas.

ELXA
And now?

DOYLE
Now I think you're a cop whose grandfather happens to be a Donohue.
(beat)
That's a very different problem.

ELXA
(a small smile)
For whom?

DOYLE
(finishing her Guinness)
For whoever's on that tape.

CUT TO:

INT. BELLE RIVE — THE KITCHEN — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 6:33 PM EST

The big Belle Rive kitchen. Nana Ruby at the stove with a pot of red beans. Natasha at the island in a cashmere sweater, reading a DGH quarterly report with a glass of wine.

Robert comes in from the garage still in his coat, carrying a small white box. He sets it on the island in front of her.

ROBERT
For you.

Natasha looks at the box. Henri's Bakery.

She opens it. Cheese straws, neatly lined up.

A beat. In the background, Ruby's spoon stops moving in the pot.

NATASHA
(evenly)
Cheese straws.

ROBERT
(taking off his coat, pleasantly)
Henri's said you'd ordered a box on Monday. I thought you must be craving them.

Natasha looks up at her husband.

He holds her gaze, easy and warm. He knows. She knows he knows. He's telling her so.

NATASHA
(beat)
I was.

ROBERT
Then enjoy them.

He kisses her cheek. He goes out toward the stairs.

Natasha sits very still. She looks at the white box.

Ruby resumes stirring. She doesn't turn around.

RUBY
(to the pot)
Well.

NATASHA
(quietly)
Don't, Ruby.

RUBY
I didn't say a word.
(beat)
That man just brought the knife back to the kitchen, baby. He didn't say sorry for picking it up.

Natasha closes the box. She pushes it away across the marble.

NATASHA
(to herself)
Twenty-three years.

She picks up the quarterly report again. She doesn't read it.

CUT TO:

INT. RAVENHURST — LUCIUS'S STUDY — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 6:49 PM EST

The dark study. The fire. Lucius at his desk, his ebony cane leaning against it. Esther stands in front of the desk in a camel coat, just home.

On the desk between them, the signed option agreement for 441 and 443 Auburn Avenue.

LUCIUS
Two lots.

ESTHER
Two lots. Six-month option. Signed this afternoon. The heirs wanted cash, and nobody else asked.
(beat)
By the time the mayor announces, I want six more.

LUCIUS
(looking at the document)
Four forty-one Auburn.
(beat)
Do you know what's at four hundred Auburn?

ESTHER
The Hotel Donohue.

LUCIUS
My father used to take me there. When I was a boy. Not inside. We weren't welcome inside. We'd stand across the street on Sunday afternoons, and he'd look up at it.
(beat)
He said, "Lucius, one day we'll own the street that hotel stands on. And they'll have to walk past us to get to their front door."

Esther listens. She has never heard her father tell this story.

LUCIUS (CONT'D)
(quietly)
He died looking at that hotel. In a photograph by his bed. I kept it.

He picks up a gold pen. He signs the internal approval memo beside the option agreement. His hand is perfectly steady. A strong, clean signature.

He looks at it. So does Esther.

ESTHER
(carefully)
Your hand.

LUCIUS
(beat)
Is fine tonight.

ESTHER
Daddy—

LUCIUS
(gently, firmly)
Esther. Take the street.

She looks at him a long moment.

ESTHER
(beat)
I'll take the street.

LUCIUS
Good.
(beat)
And Esther. Crowe tells me your uncle had a drink at the Ritz on Wednesday.

ESTHER
(evenly)
He did.

LUCIUS
With whom?

A beat. Esther looks at her father's steady hand on the desk. Then at his cane.

ESTHER
(lightly)
A banker. Nobody important.

LUCIUS
(beat)
All right.

She picks up the option agreement and goes to the door. She stops there, her back to him, and closes her eyes for exactly one second.

Then she goes.

Lucius sits alone. He looks at his steady right hand.

It trembles. Just once.

CUT TO:

INT. THE OLSON ESTATE — MALLORY'S SITTING ROOM — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 7:03 PM EST

The pale-green sitting room. Mallory sits on the window seat in pajama pants and a Spelman sweatshirt, grounded, her cell phone in her hand. On her palm, the faded Sharpie is almost gone: 312...

She dials. It rings twice.

Q (V.O.)
(filtered)
This is Q.

MALLORY
(beat)
It's the one who laughed at the Minnie.

A pause. Then his voice, warmer.

Q (V.O.)
I was starting to think you washed your hand.

MALLORY
I did. I wrote it down first.

Q (V.O.)
(laughing)
Smart.

MALLORY
I'm grounded.

Q (V.O.)
For the roof?

MALLORY
For three o'clock. My father waited up on the stairs. He didn't yell.

Q (V.O.)
(beat)
That's worse.

MALLORY
That's what everybody says.

Q (V.O.)
How long?

MALLORY
Two weeks. No Velvet.

Q (V.O.)
(beat)
Okay. Then open your window.

MALLORY
What?

Q (V.O.)
Open your window.

Mallory hesitates. Then she pushes up the old sash. Cold air comes in. Faintly, from the dark street beyond the Olson Estate's iron gate and long lawn, music drifts up from a car parked on West Paces Ferry.

Minnie Riperton. "Lovin' You." Soft. Just the song. No beat under it this time.

Mallory stares out into the dark.

MALLORY
(barely)
How do you know where I live?

Q (V.O.)
You told me. On the roof. "The big white house on Paces with the ugly lions on the gate."
(beat)
They're really ugly lions.

MALLORY
(laughing, eyes shining)
My grandfather picked them.

Q (V.O.)
Your grandfather has terrible taste in lions.

The song plays on in the cold.

Q (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(quietly)
I'm not coming to the gate. I'm not going to get you in more trouble. I just wanted you to hear the song underneath.
(beat)
Two weeks. I'll wait.

MALLORY
(beat)
You still haven't told me your name.

Q (V.O.)
(beat)
When you're not grounded.

The song ends. Down on West Paces Ferry, headlights come on. A car pulls away slowly.

Mallory stands at the open window in the cold, the phone to her ear, listening to the dial tone.

MALLORY
(to herself)
Two weeks.

She doesn't close the window for a long time.

CUT TO:

INT. THE OLSON ESTATE — THE FAMILY DINING ROOM — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 7:47 PM EST

The long formal dining room, the Venetian chandelier. Tonight the whole end of the table is set: Jasmine at the head, home, in cream silk. Peter at her right. Martin at her left. Mallory, grounded, in a cardigan.

Roast chicken. Candles. Four people being very polite.

JASMINE
—so Gordon says I'm on the short list for ranking member, and I told him I'll believe it when Pelosi calls me herself.

MARTIN
She'll call.

JASMINE
From your lips.
(beat, cutting her chicken)
Martin. Tell me about Harbour Point Logistics.

Peter's fork pauses. Mallory looks up.

Martin doesn't pause at all.

MARTIN
(easily)
A customs broker in Garden City. Donohue Shipping owns it. Very dull company. I reviewed it in October.
(beat)
Grandfather asked me about it this morning too. Popular little company this week.

JASMINE
(watching him)
Why did Daddy ask?

MARTIN
He didn't say. You know Grandfather. He asks one question and listens to the whole answer.
(beat)
Why do you ask, Mother?

JASMINE
(beat)
It came up in Washington.

MARTIN
(delicately)
In the Deep Water file.

The table goes very still.

JASMINE
(slowly)
How do you know about Deep Water?

MARTIN
(sipping his sparkling water)
Mother. Everybody in Atlanta knows about Deep Water. Channel Eight's been saying "federal organized-crime probe" every night since Friday.
(beat)
And Aunt Natasha told me you called her from D.C. last week and used the word "Shipping." She was worried.

It's plausible. Every word of it. Jasmine looks at her son. She's been in Congress twenty years. She's cross-examined cabinet secretaries.

She can't find the seam.

JASMINE
(beat)
Did you find anything? In your review?

MARTIN
A company that files paperwork and loses a little money.
(beat)
I recommended we keep it. Low strategic value.

Peter's cell phone buzzes in his jacket. Everyone at the table hears it.

JASMINE
(without looking at her husband)
Answer it, Peter.

PETER
It's dinner.

JASMINE
Answer it.

A beat. Peter takes out the phone. He looks at the display. He presses the button and puts it to his ear.

PETER
(into phone)
Hello?
(beat, a little too quickly)
No. Wrong number. Lo siento.

He hangs up. He puts the phone face down beside his plate.

PETER (CONT'D)
(evenly)
Wrong number.

Jasmine looks at him. Mallory looks at him. Martin looks at his plate and cuts a precise piece of chicken, the corner of his mouth lifting very slightly.

MALLORY
(quietly)
Daddy. You said "lo siento."

PETER
(beat)
He was speaking Spanish.

JASMINE
(setting down her knife and fork)
Mallory. Martin. Would you give your father and me the room, please.

MARTIN
(rising smoothly)
Of course, Mother.

Mallory hesitates, looking at her father. Peter won't meet her eyes. She goes.

Martin follows. At the double doors, he glances back at his parents alone at the long table in the candlelight. His mother still. His father staring at the face-down phone.

He closes the doors softly. He stands in the hall a moment, listening. Nothing but silence behind the doors.

He smiles to himself and walks away.

CUT TO:

INT. THE OLSON ESTATE — THE FAMILY DINING ROOM — CONTINUOUS

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 8:04 PM EST

The long table. The candles burning down. Jasmine and Peter alone at the far end, the roast chicken going cold between them.

JASMINE
(quietly)
Twenty-four years, Peter.

PETER
I know how long it's been.

JASMINE
Then tell me who keeps calling you from Cancún.

A long silence. Peter looks at the phone face down beside his plate.

PETER
(finally)
A clinic. Clínica Esperanza. I operated there. In the summer of 2001.

JASMINE
(beat)
You told me you were at a conference in Mexico City that summer.

PETER
I was. For three days.
(beat)
I was in Cancún for nine.

Jasmine sets her napkin on the table very slowly.

JASMINE
Operating on whom?

PETER
(beat)
A patient.

JASMINE
Peter.

PETER
A patient who paid cash and didn't want his name in an American hospital's records. It happens, Jasmine. Athletes. Actors. Men with wives.
(beat)
Now a man is asking the clinic about my patient from that summer. The clinic manager is frightened. He keeps calling. I keep not answering.

JASMINE
(evenly)
Who was the patient?

PETER
(beat)
I can't tell you that.

JASMINE
Can't or won't?

PETER
(very quietly)
Can't. Not yet. Not without making you part of it.

Jasmine looks at her husband. Ten terms in Congress. She's heard every kind of evasion there is. This isn't one of them. This is fear.

JASMINE
(beat)
Is it going to hurt this family?

PETER
(beat)
I don't know.

JASMINE
Then find out.
(standing)
Before I have to find out for you.
(beat)
Because I will, Peter. I'll find out the way I find out everything in Washington. And you won't like how many people I ask.

She walks out. The double doors close behind her.

Peter sits alone at the long table. The candles gutter. He turns the phone over.

One new voicemail. +52 998.

He doesn't play it. He holds the phone in both hands like a man holding something that might go off.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT FIVE

INT. THE SUN DIAL RESTAURANT — WESTIN PEACHTREE PLAZA — 72ND FLOOR — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 8:32 PM EST

A revolving restaurant at the top of the tallest hotel in the Southeast. Floor-to-ceiling glass. The whole city turning slowly beneath them, a carpet of lights.

At a window table: Marc in a charcoal suit, no tie. Celeste in a red dress, her hair down. Champagne. The SUN DIAL WAITER sets down two plates of scallops and withdraws.

CELESTE
(looking out)
This is your surprise?

MARC
You said you hate surprises. So I picked the one place in Atlanta where you can see everything coming.

CELESTE
(laughing)
That's very good.

MARC
I practiced.

She laughs again. The floor turns. Out the window, Donohue Tower slides into view, then Smilley Tower beside it, lit gold.

CELESTE
(watching the two towers)
From up here they look like they're about to kiss.

MARC
(beat)
From up here everything looks like it's about to kiss. That's the point of the restaurant.

She smiles. Her small evening bag sits on the table beside her plate. Tucked in its inside pocket, visible for a moment as she takes out her lipstick: a folded yellow Post-it. "SAV. PORT — DURBAN — ATTY OF RECORD."

She snaps the bag shut.

CELESTE
Marc. Can I ask you something?

MARC
You're going to anyway.

CELESTE
Does your father's firm do work for Donohue Shipping?

Marc's champagne glass stops an inch from his mouth. Just an inch. He sets it down.

MARC
(easily)
Probably. Dad's firm does work for everybody in the family. He handled my uncle Alvin's speeding tickets for ten years. That's practically a department.

CELESTE
(laughing)
Ten years?

MARC
Miami has a lot of highways.
(beat)
Why do you ask?

CELESTE
(lightly)
No reason. Something somebody said at the station.

MARC
Who?

CELESTE
(beat)
Just somebody.

The floor turns. Out the window, the dark expanse of the West End slides past.

MARC
(quietly)
Celeste. You'd tell me. If you were working on something about my family.

CELESTE
(beat)
Would you tell me? If there was something about your family I should know?

They look at each other across the candle. Two people who like each other very much, each holding something behind their back.

MARC
(finally, smiling)
Let's not talk about work.

CELESTE
(beat, smiling back)
Your cousin said you'd say that.

Marc's smile doesn't move. But something behind his eyes does.

MARC
(lightly)
My cousin?

CELESTE
(too quickly)
Martin. I ran into him. He said you hate it when people talk about work.

MARC
(beat)
Martin's very observant.

He picks up his champagne. He drinks. The city turns beneath them.

CUT TO:

INT. FULTON COUNTY COURTHOUSE — DISTRICT ATTORNEY'S OFFICE — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 9:24 PM EST

The DA's office after hours. One lamp. Arianna at her desk in shirtsleeves, alone, pretending to read an indictment. On the corkboard, Dee grins down at her.

Her desk phone rings. She picks up.

ARIANNA
Cummings.

CARLA (V.O.)
(filtered)
Madam District Attorney. Carla Mims. I'm sorry to call so late. You said every two weeks, but I thought you'd want to know tonight.

ARIANNA
(beat)
Of course.

CARLA (V.O.)
I sat with the Divines this afternoon. Good people. Mrs. Divine makes a lemon pound cake I'd go to prison for.
(beat)
Mr. Divine told me something that's not in Ray Sams's file.

Arianna's hand tightens on the phone.

CARLA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Dee always called his father for a ride. Always. From the pay phone at the Texaco at Northside and West Paces.
(beat)
So that night, he'd have walked south on Northside from the Griers' toward the Texaco. On foot. Around two in the morning.
(beat)
It's the only road down.

ARIANNA
(barely)
Yes.

CARLA (V.O.)
So I know where he was going now. I know which way he was walking. And I know he never got to the phone.
(beat)
Somewhere on Northside Drive, between the 3100 block and West Paces Ferry, something happened to that boy.

Arianna closes her eyes.

CARLA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(gently)
I thought you'd want to know. You're the one who reopened it.

ARIANNA
(evenly, eyes still closed)
Thank you, Carla.

CARLA (V.O.)
Good night, Madam District Attorney. Go home.

The line clicks off. Arianna sets the receiver down very carefully.

She looks up at the corkboard.

ARIANNA
(barely a whisper)
"Only road down."

CUT TO:

INT. BELLE RIVE — ROBERT'S STUDY — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 10:14 PM EST

A wood-paneled study: law books floor to ceiling, a leather desk chair, framed diplomas from Morehouse and Yale Law, and a signed photo of Robert with Thurgood Marshall's widow. A fire.

Robert sits in a wingback by the fire in a cardigan, reading a brief, a glass of bourbon on the side table.

A knock. Marc leans in, still in his charcoal suit from dinner.

ROBERT
(surprised, pleased)
Well. The prodigal visits.

MARC
I was in the neighborhood.

ROBERT
You live two hundred yards away.

MARC
That's the neighborhood.

Robert laughs. He waves him in. Marc sits in the other wingback. Robert lifts the decanter. Marc shakes his head.

ROBERT
How was dinner?

MARC
Revolving.

ROBERT
The Sun Dial? You took Celeste to the Sun Dial?
(delighted)
Son, that's a statement restaurant.

MARC
It revolves. She can't walk out mid-sentence without ending up in the kitchen.

ROBERT
(laughing)
That's my boy.

MARC
(beat)
Dad. Does the firm do customs work for Donohue Shipping?

A small, surprised beat. Robert sets down the brief.

ROBERT
Since 1991. Your grandfather asked me personally. Every container Shipping brings into Savannah, our Savannah associate files the paperwork. Power of attorney, entry forms, duties. Thousands a year.
(beat)
Why?

MARC
Every container?

ROBERT
Every one. It's a volume practice. Our name's on the entry form like a rubber stamp. Nobody at the firm ever looks at what's inside the box. We don't open containers, son. We file paper.

MARC
(beat)
Who at the firm runs it?

ROBERT
A paralegal in Savannah named Doris who's been with us fourteen years and has never missed a deadline. Why are you asking?

Marc looks at the fire.

MARC
Somebody at the station asked Celeste. About the firm and Shipping. She asked me at dinner.

ROBERT
(beat)
Who at the station?

MARC
She wouldn't say.

Robert takes that in. He picks up his bourbon. He's a very good lawyer, and he's hearing what his son isn't saying.

ROBERT
(quietly)
Marc. Is somebody coming after the firm?

MARC
(beat)
I don't know yet.

ROBERT
But you think so.

MARC
I think somebody might want it to look like the firm did something it didn't.
(beat)
I think you should have Doris pull every Harbour Point filing since October. Quietly. And put them somewhere safe.

Robert studies his son for a long moment. Marc holds his gaze.

ROBERT
(slowly)
How would you know the name Harbour Point?

MARC
(evenly)
Granddaddy asked me about it Monday.

It's not quite an answer. Robert knows it's not quite an answer. He lets it go the way Victor let it go. The family way.

ROBERT
(beat)
All right. Doris. Quietly.

MARC
Thank you.

He stands. At the door, he stops.

MARC (CONT'D)
Dad.

ROBERT
Hmm.

MARC
(beat)
Are you and Mama okay?

Robert's glass stops halfway to his mouth.

ROBERT
(beat, lightly)
Why does everybody in this house keep asking me that?

MARC
Because Nana Ruby's been humming "Precious Lord" all week. She only hums "Precious Lord" when somebody's in trouble.

Robert looks at the fire. For a moment, he looks very tired.

ROBERT
(quietly)
Go home, son.

Marc looks at his father. Then he goes.

Robert sits alone by the fire. He finishes the bourbon.

CUT TO:

EXT. BELLE RIVE — THE GARDEN PATH — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 10:41 PM EST

The dark garden between Belle Rive and the Pavilion. Boxwood hedges, a frozen fountain, gas lamps. Marc walks home with his hands in his pockets, his breath fogging.

His cell phone rings. He looks at it. GRANDDADDY.

MARC
(answering)
Granddaddy. You're up late.

INTERCUT WITH:

^INT. DONOHUE MANOR — THE MASTER SUITE — NIGHT

Victor sits on the edge of the four-poster in his robe, speaking quietly so as not to wake Joan, who sleeps behind him.

VICTOR
(low)
I'm sixty-eight. Everything's late.
(beat)
Harbour Point.

Marc stops walking.

MARC
Yes, sir.

VICTOR
Martin requested the file in October. He says for a portfolio review. He brought me the review. It's very good work. He signs everything "M."
(beat)
He had his answer ready before I finished the question.

MARC
(beat)
What about the bank letter?

A pause on the line.

VICTOR
(slowly)
I didn't tell you about a bank letter, Marc-Anthony.

Marc closes his eyes in the dark garden. A mistake. His first in a long time.

MARC
(evenly)
You told me you'd know by Friday. I figured whoever used your name for a file used it for more than a file.

A long silence on the line. Victor looks at the fire in the suite.

VICTOR
(finally)
That's a good guess.

MARC
Yes, sir.

VICTOR
(beat)
Men with answers ready worry me, son.
(beat)
So do men with guesses that good.

MARC
(quietly)
I know.

VICTOR
(beat)
I've decided not to ask.

MARC
Thank you.

VICTOR
Don't thank me. I'm getting old, and I'm running out of things I've decided not to ask.

He hangs up.

In the garden, Marc stands alone by the frozen fountain. He looks up at the lit window of the Manor across the dark lawn.

MARC
(to himself)
Sloppy.

He walks on.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT SIX

INT. THE BULLOCK PAVILION — MARC'S STUDY — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 11:07 PM EST

The glass study on the bluff. The chessboard. Alexander's watch ticking on the desk.

Marc sits at the desk with his tie off. Across from him, Keisha sits in the leather chair with her knees up, a laptop balanced on them, a manila folder on the desk between them.

The folder tab reads: MIMS, CARLA ANN.

KEISHA
Everything's public. I didn't have to go anywhere I'm not supposed to.
(beat)
Born March 22, 1957, in Thomson, Georgia. Married Lewis Mims, 1981. He's a retired MARTA mechanic, if you can believe it. No kids. One nephew she put through Georgia Tech.
(beat)
Twenty-two years at the GBI. Eleven cold cases closed that everybody else had quit on. The Cobb County nurse in '97, off a single Kroger receipt. The two little girls in Valdosta in 2001, off a car seat.
(beat)
First Baptist Decatur. Second pew on the left. Sings alto.

MARC
(looking at the folder, not opening it)
And how she takes her coffee?

KEISHA
Sanka. From a thermos. Two sugars.

Marc nods slowly.

KEISHA (CONT'D)
(carefully)
Marc. I've got an idea. You're not going to like it.

MARC
Say it anyway.

KEISHA
She's canvassing. She's working the valets and the cops who worked the door. She's going to find somebody who saw a car on Northside at two in the morning.
(beat)
What if she found somebody else first? A witness. A man who remembers a young guy walking on Moores Mill that night, getting into a car with some friends. Laughing. Heading toward Bankhead.
(beat)
It'd take one phone call. One man. We'd pay him well. She'd spend a year chasing a car that doesn't exist, and she'd never look at Northside again.

Silence. The watch ticks.

Marc looks at Keisha for a long time.

MARC
(quietly)
No.

KEISHA
Marc—

MARC
No.
(beat)
That family sat at their kitchen table this afternoon and fed her pound cake. Calvin Divine has driven the same bus route for three years looking at every stop for his son. Loretta pays a pager bill every month in case he calls.
(beat)
If I send Carla Mims down a road that doesn't exist, I'm not protecting Ari. I'm doing to them what somebody did to Lamar Whitlock. Making up a story on a man who can't answer.

KEISHA
(beat)
And Ari?

MARC
(beat)
I'll protect Ari. I've always protected Ari.
(beat)
But not like that. Not by lying to his mother again.

Keisha closes the laptop.

KEISHA
Then what do we do?

MARC
(beat)
Then we let her look.

KEISHA
(quietly)
And if she finds it?

Marc looks out the glass wall at the dark river below the bluff.

MARC
Then she'll find it.
(beat)
And I'll deal with what happens after. Me. Not Ari.

Keisha studies him. She's known him since he was eighteen.

KEISHA
(softly)
That's the most dangerous thing I've ever heard you say.

MARC
(beat)
I know.

He picks up the folder labeled MIMS, CARLA ANN. He looks at it. Then he opens the bottom drawer of his desk and puts it inside, unread.

He closes the drawer.

MARC (CONT'D)
Go home, Ghost. Get some sleep. You've been awake for two days.

KEISHA
(standing, pulling up her hood)
You too.

She goes. Marc sits alone with the ticking watch.

He picks it up. He turns it over. "A. J. D. — 1936 — They closed every door."

He holds it in his palm and listens to it.

CUT TO:

INT. A MIDTOWN PARKING GARAGE — LEVEL 6 — ROOF — NIGHT

~ Friday, January 14, 2005 — 11:48 PM EST

The open top level of an empty office-building garage. Cold wind. Sodium lights, half of them out. The glass towers of Midtown rise all around, most windows dark.

A black BMW 7 Series sits alone near the stairwell. Martin stands beside it in his charcoal overcoat, gloved hands in his pockets, perfectly still.

Footsteps on the stairwell. Unhurried.

A man steps out onto the roof. Tall. Broad-shouldered. A dark canvas jacket and a baseball cap pulled low. He walks straight to Martin, the same measured pace, arms quiet at his sides.

He stops six feet away. He takes off the cap.

CYRUS KNOX (36). White, sandy hair cut short, a long jaw, a weathered face that's spent years in harder sun than Georgia's. Pale, patient eyes. He looks like a man who has never once been in a hurry.

KNOX
(soft South African accent)
Mr. Olson.

MARTIN
You're on time.

KNOX
I'm always on time. It's the only thing people remember about me, and I prefer it that way.

MARTIN
The Chief knows the coins are fake. The whole city knows she knows. Channel Eight says so every hour.

KNOX
I watched.

MARTIN
And the FBI's lab says your stone came from the Northern Cape.

KNOX
(unbothered)
Half the onyx in the world comes from the Northern Cape. That tells them nothing they can use.
(beat)
And a New Zealand license tells them nothing either. Not for long.

MARTIN
(beat)
They'll find Cameron Kerr eventually.

KNOX
Cameron Kerr left the country this morning. Someone else came back in.

Martin almost smiles.

MARTIN
The Merriweather nephew.

KNOX
Monday. He's angry and he's young. He'll listen.

MARTIN
Good.

He takes one gloved hand out of his coat pocket. In it, a thick, plain brown envelope, sealed. He sets it on the hood of the BMW and slides it across the cold black paint toward Knox.

Knox doesn't look at it. He looks at Martin.

MARTIN (CONT'D)
(quietly)
A deacon and a loan shark. They buried them on a Saturday and the city went back to brunch.
(beat)
Nobody north of I-20 is afraid yet.

KNOX
No.

MARTIN
(evenly)
Again.
(beat)
Bigger.

A long beat. The wind moves over the empty roof. Far off, a siren winds down Peachtree and fades.

Knox picks up the envelope. He weighs it once in his palm without opening it. He tucks it inside his jacket.

KNOX
(quietly)
Someone they can't ignore.

MARTIN
(beat)
Someone they can't call a criminal.

Knox studies the young man in front of him for a moment, with something that might be professional respect.

KNOX
(putting the cap back on, pulling it low)
You're very young to be this patient, Mr. Olson.

MARTIN
(pleasantly)
I had a very good teacher.

Knox nods once. He turns and walks back toward the stairwell at the same even pace. His footsteps fade down the concrete stairs.

Martin stands alone on the roof beside the black car.

He looks out over the city. Beyond the Midtown towers, far to the north, the lights of Buckhead. And somewhere beyond them, the gates of Belmont Crest.

He takes out the black phone with no display. He dials a long international number. He waits.

MARTIN (CONT'D)
(into phone, softly)
It's done.
(beat)
Next week.
(listening, a small smile)
Yes. Exactly as you said it would.

He hangs up.

HOLD on Martin Olson, alone on the roof in the cold, smiling at the city he intends to own.

FADE OUT.

> END OF EPISODE #0010 <

===

> NEXT ON BEYOND THE KINGDOM... <

!— It's MLK DAY at Greater Crest Tabernacle, and BISHOP LYONS honors JOAN DONOHUE's civil-rights legacy before the whole congregation.

!— In the back pew, ESTHER SMILLEY slips out a side door just as MARC walks in late.

!— MARC stops in the aisle. The scent of jasmine. He turns, but there's only a door swinging closed.

> FADE TO BLACK. <
